    "bottomLine": "The mold killer we would reach for first: a genuine EPA-registered fungicide with clear directions, gentle enough for wood and drywall.",
    "description": "RMR-141 is the product we would use to actually kill mold on a surface, and it earns that place because its claims rest on an EPA registration rather than marketing. The registration number, 61178-2-93443, appears on its safety data sheet, and the active ingredients are two quaternary ammonium compounds at 0.07 percent each, the same class of chemistry used in many hospital disinfectants. RMR Solutions says it kills over 140 microorganisms, including black, grey and green mold. The directions are the part that matters. To treat mold, you saturate the surface and keep it wet for 10 minutes, then wipe away the excess or let it air dry. That contact time is what the kill claim depends on, so spraying and wiping straight away does not do the job. Because it is not bleach, it does not whiten fabrics or attack most finishes, and its safety data sheet lists only mild eye irritation as a hazard. What it will not do is remove staining. Dead mold can still leave dark marks in grout or wood, which is why RMR sells a separate stain remover. Use RMR-141 to kill and RMR-86 or scrubbing to clean up what is left."
