    "description": "For the black spots that colonise shower grout and caulk lines, a bleach-based spray is genuinely the fastest fix, and Clorox Plus Tilex Mold & Mildew Remover is the one most households already know. Its active ingredient is sodium hypochlorite, and Clorox says it kills 99.9 percent of mold and mildew. You spray it on, let the stains disappear, and rinse. On glazed tile and grout the stains often fade while you watch. The product page is unusually clear about where not to use it. It is approved for grout, glazed ceramic tile, tubs, fiberglass, glass shower doors, vinyl curtains, counters, sinks and no-wax floors. It should not be used on wood or painted surfaces, aluminum, fabric, carpet or paper, and should be rinsed promptly off rubber, plastic and vinyl. That list is the whole argument for this page: bleach is a non-porous surface product. Clorox also notes the sodium hypochlorite loses strength after about a year, so an old bottle under the sink may not be doing much. Ventilate the bathroom and never mix it with toilet bowl cleaners, rust removers, acids or ammonia, because the combination releases dangerous fumes."
