        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does bleach kill mold?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "On hard, non-porous surfaces like tile and glass, bleach kills surface mold and removes stains. On porous materials it mostly whitens the stain and adds moisture, leaving growth below the surface. The EPA does not recommend biocides like bleach as a routine part of mold cleanup, and bleach must never be mixed with ammonia."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I remove mold myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually, if the total area is under about 10 square feet, the EPA's rough limit for do-it-yourself cleanup. Wear an N-95 respirator, long gloves and sealed goggles, fix the moisture source, scrub hard surfaces, remove moldy porous materials and dry everything. Call a professional for larger areas, HVAC mold or contaminated water."
          }
        },
