        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="three-jobs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Kill, Remove or Prevent: Three Different Jobs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The shelf labelled mold removers holds three different kinds of product, and buying the wrong one is the most common reason a mold problem comes back. Some products kill mold. Some remove the stains it leaves. Some leave the surface resistant to regrowth. A few claim to do two or three of these at once. Knowing which job you need done, and reading the label to see which job a product actually claims, matters more than any brand name.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mold killers are pesticides in the legal sense, because they claim to kill a living organism, and in the United States any product making that claim must be registered with the EPA and carry an EPA registration number on the label. RMR-141 is an example: a registered fungicide and disinfectant with a defined contact time. Stain removers, such as RMR-86, use bleach to whiten the dark pigment mold leaves behind, and some say clearly that they do not kill mold at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            Preventers and inhibitors, such as Concrobium Mold Control, are applied and left on the surface to discourage regrowth, and are most useful after cleanup or across large areas like crawlspaces. There is also a fourth category that is not really a product at all: plain detergent and water. For mold on hard surfaces, the EPA's guidance is to scrub it off with detergent and water and dry the area completely, and it does not recommend routinely using biocides like bleach during cleanup.
          </p>

          <p>
            That last point frames this whole guide. No spray fixes a mold problem on its own. Mold grows where there is moisture, and if the moisture stays, the mold returns whatever you put on it. Our guide to getting rid of mold in a basement walks through the full process. The products below are tools for specific parts of that job, and the sections after them explain which to use where.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We built this list from labels, safety data sheets and manufacturers' own directions rather than marketing copy, because mold products are full of claims that sound similar and mean very different things. For each product, we checked what it actually claims to do, whether it carries an EPA registration where it makes a kill claim, what the active ingredient is, how long it must stay wet to work, which surfaces it is approved for, and what protective equipment the maker recommends.
          </p>

          <p>
            We gave priority to products that are honest about their limits. RMR states that its stain remover does not kill mold. Clorox lists the surfaces its Tilex spray must not be used on. Mold Armor states its product is for hard, non-porous surfaces. That kind of clarity is worth more than a bold promise, because using the right product on the right surface is the whole game.
          </p>

          <p>
            We also wanted coverage of the real situations people face: black mildew in a shower, a stained basement wall, a damp crawlspace, mildew on siding and decks, and the aftermath of a small leak. No single product covers all of them well. The six picks below are chosen so that between them, and with detergent, water and a dehumidifier, you can handle every small mold job a homeowner should take on alone.
          </p>

