        <div id="labels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Read a Mold Product Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the EPA registration number, usually printed as EPA Reg. No. followed by a series of digits. If a product claims to kill mold, mildew, bacteria or viruses, that number should be there. RMR-141's is 61178-2-93443. You can look up a registration on the EPA's pesticide product label system to see the approved label. If a bottle promises to kill mold and has no registration number, treat the claim with suspicion.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, find the active ingredient and its concentration. Bleach-based products list sodium hypochlorite; RMR-86's safety data sheet gives it as 1 to 5 percent. Many registered disinfectants use quaternary ammonium compounds, which RMR-141 contains at 0.07 percent each of two types. Botanical products often use thymol from thyme oil. The active ingredient tells you how the product behaves: bleach whitens and corrodes, quats do not whiten, and botanicals tend to be milder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The contact time, sometimes called dwell time, is the most overlooked line on the label. It is how long the surface must stay visibly wet for the kill claim to hold. RMR-141 calls for 10 minutes of saturation for mold. Mold Armor Rapid Clean Remediation claims 60 seconds. Spray-and-wipe use that ignores the contact time does not deliver the kill the label promises, so on vertical surfaces you may need to re-spray to keep it wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, read the surfaces and precautions. The label tells you where a product can be used and where it will cause damage, what protective equipment to wear, and what must never be mixed with it. The signal word, Caution, Warning or Danger, indicates the level of acute hazard. With pesticide products, the label is a legal document, and using a product in a way that contradicts it is both less effective and, technically, a violation of federal law.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="porous" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Porous vs Non-Porous Surfaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whether a surface is porous decides whether any product can fix it. On non-porous materials such as glazed tile, glass, metal, sealed countertops, plastic and fiberglass, mold grows on the surface. It can be scrubbed off, the surface disinfected if you choose, and the job is done once the area is dry. Any of the indoor products on this list will work, with bleach sprays especially quick on tile and grout.
          </p>

          <p>
            Porous materials such as drywall, ceiling tiles, carpet, insulation, upholstery and paper absorb water, and mold grows into them, not just onto them. The EPA advises that absorbent materials that become moldy may need to be thrown away, because mold can fill the empty spaces and crevices and be impossible to remove completely. A spray may kill what it reaches and make the stain disappear, but the growth inside the material remains.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Examples</th>
