                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Semi-porous materials are the grey zone. Framing lumber, subfloor, concrete and grout can usually be saved. Scrub them, treat them with a registered fungicide, and let them dry completely. On wood, if staining or growth remains in the grain after it dries, sanding or wire brushing removes it, with a respirator on and a HEPA vacuum close by. A stain-blocking primer can then seal what remains of the discoloration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drywall is where people most often get it wrong. A small surface spot on painted drywall in a dry room can be cleaned and treated. A patch that has been wet, is soft, or shows growth on the paper facing should be cut out, generally a foot or so beyond the visible edge, and replaced after the cavity behind it is dry. Spraying it and painting over the stain only hides the problem until the next damp spell.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bleach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Truth About Bleach and Vinegar
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bleach is the first thing most people grab, and on hard, non-porous surfaces it does kill surface mold and remove staining quickly, which is why bleach sprays are a reasonable choice for shower grout. But the EPA does not recommend biocides like chlorine bleach as a routine part of mold cleanup, and on porous materials bleach gives a misleading result: it whitens the stain so the surface looks clean while doing little about growth below the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            Household bleach is mostly water. Soaking drywall or wood with it adds moisture to the material you are trying to dry, and it can damage finishes, corrode metal and fade fabrics. It also loses strength over time; Clorox notes the sodium hypochlorite in its Tilex spray loses strength after about a year. If you do use bleach, use it on hard surfaces, follow the label dilution, ventilate the room, and never mix it with ammonia or acidic cleaners.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vinegar is the other household favourite. Its acetic acid has some effect on mold, and it is a reasonable mild cleaner for light mildew on non-porous surfaces. It is not an EPA-registered mold killer, so there is no approved contact time or kill claim to rely on, and it is not a substitute for drying the area. Never combine vinegar with bleach: the mixture releases chlorine gas. Mixing vinegar and hydrogen peroxide in one container also creates a corrosive acid.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is simple. For shower grout and tile, a bleach spray is fine. For wood, concrete and drywall, scrub with detergent and water, use a registered non-bleach fungicide such as RMR-141 if you want to treat the surface, then use a stain remover only on the marks that remain and only where bleach will not cause damage. Dry everything thoroughly afterwards; drying is the step that actually prevents regrowth.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fix the Moisture First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mold needs moisture, and every mold problem is really a moisture problem with a symptom on the surface. The EPA's guidance starts with finding and fixing the water source, whether that is a plumbing leak, a roof leak, groundwater coming through a foundation, condensation on cold walls or simply humid air. Clean the mold without fixing the source and it returns, often within weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoor humidity is the most common cause in basements and bathrooms. The EPA recommends keeping relative humidity below 60 percent and ideally between 30 and 50 percent. In a basement, that usually means running a dehumidifier through the humid months; our guide to the <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best basement dehumidifiers</a> covers sizing, and our article on dehumidifier settings covers where to set it. In bathrooms, a properly sized exhaust fan run for 20 minutes after showers does the same job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure rather than guess. A cheap hygrometer tells you room humidity, and a moisture meter tells you whether a wall, floor or piece of framing is actually wet. That matters after cleanup, because you should not close up a wall or repaint until the material has dried to normal levels. Our guide to the best moisture meters explains pin and pinless types and the readings that mean dry for wood and drywall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed matters after any leak or flood. The EPA's window for drying water-damaged materials is 24 to 48 hours, after which mold growth becomes likely. Run fans and a dehumidifier, remove wet carpet pad and soaked insulation, and open up wet wall cavities if needed. In attics, mold on the roof sheathing usually traces back to warm, moist house air leaking upward; our attic fan guide explains why air sealing the ceiling matters more than adding a fan.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Safety: Respirators, Ventilation and Never Mixing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mold cleanup disturbs spores and fragments, and some people react strongly to them, especially those with asthma, allergies or weakened immune systems. The EPA's minimum kit for a small cleanup is an N-95 respirator, gloves that reach the middle of the forearm, and goggles without ventilation holes. A loose paper dust mask does very little. If anyone in the house has respiratory problems, they should stay away from the area while you work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chemical safety is the second layer. Bleach-based products such as Tilex and RMR-86 release strong fumes, and RMR recommends a full face respirator with the appropriate cartridges for its stain remover, along with skin protection. Open windows, run an exhaust fan, and take breaks. Read each label's signal word and precautions before you start rather than after you smell something.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never mix cleaning products. Bleach mixed with ammonia produces toxic chloramine vapours, and bleach mixed with acids, including vinegar and many toilet bowl and rust cleaners, releases chlorine gas. Clorox warns against mixing Tilex with toilet bowl cleaners, rust removers, acids or ammonia. Even using two products one after another on the same surface can mix them, so rinse thoroughly between products and use one at a time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Contain the work. Close doors to the rest of the house, cover vents in the room, and bag moldy debris before carrying it through living areas. Clean up dust with a HEPA vacuum rather than a regular one, which can blow fine particles around. An air purifier with a true HEPA filter can capture airborne spores during and after the work; our guide to the best air purifiers for mold covers what they can and cannot do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-by-step" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How to Clean Mold Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            First, find and stop the moisture source, and decide whether the job is yours. If the moldy area is under about 10 square feet, roughly a three-by-three-foot patch, the EPA says you can in most cases handle it yourself. Larger areas, mold in the heating and cooling system, mold after sewage or contaminated flooding, and anyone with health concerns all point to a professional.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, set up. Put on the N-95, gloves and goggles, ventilate the room, close it off from the rest of the house and lay plastic under the work area. Remove and bag porous materials that are moldy, such as ceiling tiles, carpet pad and soaked drywall, before cleaning anything else, because they will keep releasing spores while you work around them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, clean. Scrub hard and semi-porous surfaces with detergent and water using a stiff brush, then wipe them down. If you want to treat the surface, apply a registered fungicide such as RMR-141 and keep it wet for the full contact time on the label. On tile and grout, a bleach spray such as Tilex does the cleaning and staining together. On wood, sand out any growth left in the grain once it is dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fourth, dry and finish. Run fans and a dehumidifier until a moisture meter shows the materials are dry, which may take days. Deal with remaining stains using a stain remover where the surface can take bleach, or a stain-blocking primer where it cannot. Apply an inhibitor such as Concrobium if the area is prone to dampness. Only then rebuild, repaint or put belongings back, and keep watching humidity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-call-a-pro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. When to Call a Pro, and What Not to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Call a professional when the moldy area is larger than about 10 square feet, when mold is inside ductwork or the air handler, when the water involved was sewage or floodwater, or when the mold keeps returning despite your cleanup. The same applies if anyone in the house has asthma, a weakened immune system or symptoms that seem to track the problem. A good remediator will find the moisture source, contain the area properly and remove damaged materials rather than just spraying them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary of anyone whose main tool is a fogger or a machine. Ozone generators are sold as mold cures, but ozone at levels safe for people does little to mold, and at levels high enough to affect it, ozone is a lung irritant; our article on whether ozone generators kill mold covers the evidence. UV lamps have similar limits, because they only affect what the light directly reaches, as explained in our guide to whether UV light kills mold.
          </p>

          <p>
            Air purifiers are useful for capturing airborne spores, but they do not remove mold growing on a surface. They are a support tool during and after cleanup, not a treatment. Our article on whether air purifiers help with mold explains where they fit. The same goes for any product, however good, that promises to fix mold without anyone fixing the water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mold testing kits are another common waste. If you can see or smell mold, you already know it is there, and the EPA notes that sampling is usually unnecessary for deciding to clean it up. Spend the money on a dehumidifier, a moisture meter and the right cleaning product instead. Testing has its place, for example to check a professional's work or in legal disputes, but it does not make the mold go away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting Over Mold</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Covering mold with paint, even a mold-resistant paint, hides it rather than removing it, and growth often reappears through the new coat. Clean and dry the surface first, treat it if needed, and only then use a stain-blocking primer and paint. Fix the moisture source, or none of it lasts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Bleach on Drywall and Wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bleach whitens the stain on porous materials so the surface looks clean, while adding water and leaving growth below the surface. On drywall and wood, scrub with detergent and water, use a registered non-bleach fungicide if needed, and replace drywall that is soft or heavily stained.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Contact Time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A mold killer only delivers its label claim if the surface stays wet for the full contact time, such as 10 minutes for RMR-141. Spraying and wiping immediately leaves the job half done. On walls and ceilings, re-spray as needed to keep the area visibly wet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Products</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bleach mixed with ammonia releases toxic vapours, and bleach mixed with vinegar or acidic cleaners releases chlorine gas. Use one product at a time, rinse thoroughly between products, and keep the room ventilated. Read the warnings on each label before you start, not after something smells wrong.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cleaning Without Fixing the Moisture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every mold problem is a moisture problem first. Clean without fixing the leak, condensation or high humidity, and mold returns within weeks. Find the source, keep indoor humidity below 60 percent and ideally 30 to 50, and dry wet materials within 24 to 48 hours.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

