        <PlanTheJob questions={false} />

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mold remover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the job. For killing mold, RMR-141, an EPA-registered quaternary ammonium fungicide, is our top pick. For shower grout and tile, a bleach spray such as Clorox Plus Tilex works fastest. For stains, RMR-86. For prevention and large areas, Concrobium Mold Control. Outdoors on siding and decks, Wet & Forget Outdoor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mold killer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Choose an EPA-registered fungicide and follow its contact time. RMR-141 is registered, uses quaternary ammonium rather than bleach, and requires 10 minutes of wet contact for mold. On hard surfaces, Mold Armor Rapid Clean Remediation claims a 60-second kill. Remember that killing mold does not remove the stains it leaves behind.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does bleach kill mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On hard, non-porous surfaces like tile and glass, bleach kills surface mold and removes stains. On porous materials it mostly whitens the stain and adds moisture, leaving growth below the surface. The EPA does not recommend biocides like bleach as a routine part of mold cleanup, and bleach must never be mixed with ammonia.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I remove mold myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, if the total area is under about 10 square feet, the EPA's rough limit for do-it-yourself cleanup. Wear an N-95 respirator, long gloves and sealed goggles, fix the moisture source, scrub hard surfaces, remove moldy porous materials and dry everything. Call a professional for larger areas, HVAC mold or contaminated water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is vinegar better than bleach for mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Neither is ideal. Vinegar can clean light mildew on hard surfaces, but it is not an EPA-registered mold killer, so there is no approved contact time or kill claim. Bleach works on non-porous surfaces but not on porous ones. Never mix the two, because the combination releases toxic chlorine gas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kills mold on wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Scrub with detergent and water, then apply a registered non-bleach fungicide such as RMR-141 and keep it wet for its full contact time. Let the wood dry completely, check it with a moisture meter, and sand out any growth left in the grain. Seal remaining staining with a stain-blocking primer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I stop mold from coming back?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fix the moisture. Keep indoor humidity below 60 percent and ideally 30 to 50, run bathroom fans after showers, repair leaks quickly and dry wet materials within 24 to 48 hours. An inhibitor such as Concrobium Mold Control can help on surfaces prone to dampness, but humidity control does most of the work.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
